## Root Cause

The bug is on **line 39** of `include/ringbuffer.hpp`:

```cpp
std::size_t size() const { return tail_ - head_; }
```

This computation only works when `tail_ >= head_` — i.e., when the write position has not yet passed the read position in the circular array. Once the buffer wraps around, `tail_` can become **less than** `head_`. Because both are `std::size_t` (unsigned), `tail_ - head_` underflows and wraps around to a huge number (e.g., `SIZE_MAX - (head_ - tail_) + 1`).

### Why it also returns 0 when full

When the buffer is full, `tail_ == head_` (the invariant that makes `full_` true — see line 19). So `tail_ - head_` is literally `0`, even though the buffer holds `N` elements.

### Correct implementation

The size must account for wrapping. Either of these fixes works:

```cpp
// Option 1: conditional
std::size_t size() const {
    if (tail_ >= head_) return tail_ - head_;
    return N - (head_ - tail_);
}

// Option 2: modular arithmetic (equivalent, single expression)
std::size_t size() const { return (tail_ - head_ + N) % N; }
```

Both produce the correct count in all states: empty (0), partially full, and full (N).

